Q: Material-Ui DatePicker change to string A: <p>I've just tackled a similar issue. Where if the selected day is today (i.e. new Date()), then DatePicker should show string 'Today'.</p>
<p>For that to work you should create a helper function to format the value. Also, I'm using react-hook-form, but that's up to you.</p>
<pre><code>const formatDate = (date: Date | null) =&gt; {
  if (date &amp;&amp; isSameDay(date, TODAY)) {
    return 'Today';
  }
  return date ? format(date, 'MM/dd/yyyy') : '';
};
</code></pre>
<p>It will be controlled component:</p>
<pre><code>const [startDate, setStartDate] = useState&lt;Date | null&gt;(TODAY);
</code></pre>
<p>Main focus should be on slotProps &amp; inputProps:</p>
<pre><code>         &lt;Controller
            control={control}
            name=&quot;startDate&quot;
            rules={{
              required: isWeekly,
            }}
            render={({ field, fieldState }) =&gt; (
              &lt;DatePicker
                {...field}
                sx={{ flex: 1 }}
                disablePast
                label={t('dashboard.newScraper.scheduling.startDate')}
                value={field.value || startDate}
                onChange={newValue =&gt; {
                  field.onChange(newValue);
                  setStartDate(newValue);
                }}
                slots={{
                  openPickerIcon: CalendarTodayIcon,
                }}
                slotProps={{
                  textField: {
                    variant: 'outlined',
                    error: Boolean(fieldState.error),
                    helperText:
                      fieldState.error &amp;&amp;
                      t(`common.errors.${fieldState.error.type}`, { defaultValue: fieldState.error.message }),
                    inputProps: {
                      value: formatDate(field.value) || formatDate(startDate),
                    },
                  },
                }}
              /&gt;
            )}
          /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And the cool part that the DatePicker always returns Date object.</p>

Q: How to calculate CRC for ISO CAN-FD frames A: <p>I know that this question already has an answer. But this question on StackOverflow is the only page in the entire internet where a code with example data for CAN bus CRC calculation can be found!</p>
<p>I found theoretical descriptions how to calculate the CAN bus CRC, but neither working code nor any example data. I also asked AI which had no clue how to calculate this. It gave me wrong formulas. I was really stuck with my project.</p>
<p>The reason why this is so difficult is that nobody in the real world has to manually calculate the CAN bus CRC. This is done in hardware in the processors that support CAN bus communication, so no embedded programmer has to ever care about this.</p>
<p>Additionally the ISO documents where the CRC calculation is explained cannot be downloaded, except you pay for them.</p>
<p>I need this because I'am writing an application that converts an  oscilloscope into a logic analyzer. It loads a capture over USB from the oscilloscope to the computer and has a lot of decoders for UART, SPI, I2C and also CAN bus, that analyze the signal bit by bit.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/jtKEP7GF.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/jtKEP7GF.png" alt="Oszi Waveform Analyzer - CAN FD decoder" /></a></p>
<p>This allows to find errors on a CAN bus using an oscilloscope. It checks for all possible errors (sample point timing, stuff bits, parity bits, CRC) so I need to manually calculate the CRC for 15 bit, 17 bit and 21 bit CAN bus packets. It took me a long time to get the CRC calculation working correctly.</p>
<p>If you come here from Google, like me, and need sample data and working code, download my <strong>open-source project</strong> from <a href="https://netcult.ch/elmue/Oszi-Waveform-Analyzer/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://netcult.ch/elmue/Oszi-Waveform-Analyzer/</a> It is full of demo files with real CAN bus data captures from CAN classic and CAN FD. All demos result in a correct checksum.</p>
<p>The Python code here in the question is surely not written by an experienced programmer, who would never write bits into an array of 17 integers and roll the array members around. Obviously 17 bits can be written into one integer and calculation can be done by bit shifting and XOR operations, which the processor does in one single instruction.</p>
<p>I wrote a small speed-optimized class in C#, that does the CRC calculation for CAN classic and CAN FD:</p>
<pre class="lang-csharp prettyprint-override"><code>/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// See &quot;Bosch CAN FD.pdf&quot; page 14 and &quot;ISO 11898-1 (2015) CRC.png&quot; 
/// in subfolder &quot;Documentation&quot; of Oszi Waveform Analyzer.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
class CanCrc
{
    int ms32_CRC, ms32_Poly, ms32_HiBit, ms32_Mask;

    // Constructor
    public CanCrc(CanPacket i_Packet)
    {
        int s32_CrcLen = 15;
        if (i_Packet.IsCanFD)
            s32_CrcLen = i_Packet.ms32_DLC &gt; 16 ? 21 : 17;

        ms32_HiBit =  1 &lt;&lt; (s32_CrcLen - 1); // 15 bit --&gt; 0x4000
        ms32_Mask  = (1 &lt;&lt; s32_CrcLen) - 1;  // 15 bit --&gt; 0x7FFF

        switch (s32_CrcLen)
        {
            case 15: ms32_Poly = 0x004599; ms32_CRC = 0;          break; // CAN classic
            case 17: ms32_Poly = 0x01685B; ms32_CRC = ms32_HiBit; break; // CAN FD
            case 21: ms32_Poly = 0x102899; ms32_CRC = ms32_HiBit; break; // CAN FD
        }
    }

    // Shift-in the next bit
    public void Calculate(bool b_Bit)
    {
        bool b_Highest = (ms32_CRC &amp; ms32_HiBit) &gt; 0;
        ms32_CRC &lt;&lt;= 1;
        if (b_Highest ^ b_Bit)
            ms32_CRC  ^= ms32_Poly; 
    }

    // Get the finished CRC
    public int Finish()
    {
        return ms32_CRC &amp; ms32_Mask;
    }
}
</code></pre>
